os_linux_arm: build with bionic and clang

Three things in the arm32 os layer that only ever saw glibc + gcc:

  * <fpu_control.h> is a glibc header; bionic has none. The two users here,
    os::Linux::get/set_fpu_control_word(), are empty stubs on arm, so the
    include is dead weight on android -- guard it.
  * os::current_stack_pointer() declares a GNU "register asm" local. C++17
    dropped the register storage class and clang rejects it; read sp with a
    plain asm output constraint instead.
  * setup_fpu()'s inline asm spells the register "%%r0". That is extended-asm
    escaping for a "%r0" the GNU assembler tolerates; clang's integrated
    assembler does not. Write it as "r0".

Matches the fixes termux-packages carries for openjdk on android
(0013-Don-t-run-fpu_control-related-functions-on-Android,
0018-arm-fix-assembly-in-os_linux_arm.cpp, 0021-Fix-StackOverflowError-on-ARM).

diff --git a/src/hotspot/os_cpu/linux_arm/os_linux_arm.cpp b/src/hotspot/os_cpu/linux_arm/os_linux_arm.cpp
index 3726aca..d201137 100644
--- a/src/hotspot/os_cpu/linux_arm/os_linux_arm.cpp
+++ b/src/hotspot/os_cpu/linux_arm/os_linux_arm.cpp
@@ -72,7 +72,9 @@
 # include <pwd.h>
 # include <poll.h>
 # include <ucontext.h>
+#ifndef __ANDROID__
 # include <fpu_control.h>
+#endif
 # include <asm/ptrace.h>
 
 #define SPELL_REG_SP  "sp"
@@ -90,8 +92,14 @@ enum {
 #endif
 
 address os::current_stack_pointer() {
+#if defined(__clang__) || defined(__llvm__)
+  void* sp;
+  __asm__("mov %0, " SPELL_REG_SP : "=r"(sp));
+  return (address) sp;
+#else
   register address sp __asm__ (SPELL_REG_SP);
   return sp;
+#endif
 }
 
 char* os::non_memory_address_word() {
@@ -433,8 +441,8 @@ void os::setup_fpu() {
 #if !defined(__SOFTFP__) && defined(__VFP_FP__)
   // Turn on IEEE-754 compliant VFP mode
   __asm__ volatile (
-    "mov %%r0, #0;"
-    "fmxr fpscr, %%r0"
+    "mov r0, #0;"
+    "fmxr fpscr, r0"
     : /* no output */ : /* no input */ : "r0"
   );
 #endif
